# Dispute desk
#
# Shows:   `when dk` — reacting to a DontKnowTrade — with the trade in hand,
#          trade.dk_reason and trade.dk_text, a counter kept with `let`,
#          renotify and bust
# Needs:   any session that receives orders, and a counterparty that disputes
#          (the client-side example dk-policy does)
# Watch:   Sent Trades: the DK and DK Text columns, then a new ExecID
# Outcome: a price dispute busts the trade; any other dispute is answered by
#          re-notifying once, and a second dispute busts it

macro dispute-desk

on order
    accept
    fill qty: order.order_qty, price: order.price

    # A name set with `let` belongs to this order's macro; setting it again
    # changes it, so it can count.
    let disputes = 0

    # Inside `when dk` the trade is the one disputed, so `renotify` and
    # `bust` need no target. The macro stays alive for its `when` lines
    # after the last plain line has run.
    when dk
        let disputes = disputes + 1

        if trade.dk_reason == 'PriceExceedsLimit'
            after 500ms
            bust text: 'busted: ${trade.dk_text}'

        # A re-notification goes out under a new ExecID, and the counterparty
        # records it as a new trade. A desk that re-sends every dispute,
        # facing a client that disputes every report, would go round for
        # ever: one re-send per order stops it here.
        else if disputes > 1
            bust text: 'disputed twice'
        else
            after 1s
            renotify text: 'as reported'
